> API and interpretation

SHA-1 is a historical algorithm with known practical collisions; these
conformance and sensitivity examples do not restore collision resistance. Raw
bytes include NUL/high bytes. Complete FIPS length, padded geometry and optional
capture bounds are checked before hashing. The histogram retains every distance
from zero through the full digest width, so tail values are never relabelled
into clipped bins.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [NIST, FIPS180-4 Secure Hash Standard (2015)|https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final]

- [Stevens and collaborators, Announcing the first SHA1 collision (2017)|https://security.googleblog.com/2017/02/announcing-first-sha1-collision.html]

